const faqs = [["What is terminology management?", "Terminology management is the structured process of identifying, defining, translating, approving, applying, and maintaining the words and concepts that matter to an organization. It helps teams use consistent product, technical, brand, legal, medical, financial, and regulated language across content, languages, and markets."], ["What is a termbase?", "A termbase is a structured terminology database used to manage specialized concepts and their approved language equivalents. It can preserve definitions, preferred terms, approved translations, acceptable alternatives, prohibited variants, domain information, usage guidance, ownership, approval status, and market-specific context."], ["What is the difference between a glossary and a termbase?", "A glossary is often a relatively simple list of source terms and translations. A termbase is more structured and governed, capturing the concept behind each term, context, language and locale variants, owners, status, comments, and approval history."], ["How is terminology management different from translation memory?", "Terminology management controls individual terms, names, abbreviations, and specialized concepts. Translation memory stores complete source and target segments such as sentences, headings, or interface strings. Termbases provide term-level control, while translation memories provide content-level reuse."], ["How does terminology management improve AI translation?", "Approved terminology gives AI translation workflows clearer direction for organization-specific product names, specialized vocabulary, preferred translations, and prohibited variants. Human review remains important when terminology requires grammatical adaptation, contextual interpretation, or market-specific judgment."], ["Does terminology management replace professional translators?", "No. Terminology management gives translators approved language and context, while professional linguists determine how terminology should be incorporated into complete sentences with accurate meaning, grammar, fluency, tone, and audience fit."], ["Can existing glossaries and terminology resources be imported?", "Existing glossaries, spreadsheets, legacy termbases, style guides, product documentation, translation memories, and bilingual files can be assessed, consolidated, normalized, and prepared for connected terminology workflows."], ["Can terminology vary by product, department, or market?", "Yes. The same organization may require different approved terminology for different products, subject domains, departments, audiences, countries, or regional markets. Structured scoping helps ensure the correct language resource is applied to the correct content."], ["Who should approve enterprise terminology?", "Approval should reflect the type and importance of the term. Professional linguists may approve general language, while technical, medical, legal, regulatory, brand, or regional terminology may require authorized subject-matter reviewers."], ["Can terminology QA identify incorrect usage?", "Terminology requirements can support automated and professional quality assurance by identifying missing preferred terms, prohibited variants, inconsistent translations, capitalization differences, and terms that do not match the assigned product, domain, or market."], ["How often should a termbase be updated?", "A termbase should be maintained whenever products, services, regulations, markets, content, or approved language change. Updates may follow a product launch, rebranding, quality finding, new target language, or correction approved during translation review."], ["Can Stepes help build a new terminology program?", "Yes. Stepes can help assess existing language assets, identify priority terminology, consolidate conflicting resources, extract candidate terms, structure multilingual entries, coordinate professional validation, and establish workflows for ongoing maintenance."]];

